The arm-selection gate was `command -v gh` -- "is the binary installed" --
where the question it needed answered was "can gh complete a request".

Those came apart in practice. Ubuntu's packaged gh (2.45.0-1ubuntu0.3)
emits a malformed User-Agent -- `GitHub CLI ` with a trailing space, the
version never injected -- and api.github.com rejects it as an HTTP/2
PROTOCOL_ERROR. Every gh API call fails on that build while `command -v
gh` happily succeeds, so contribute took the gh arm and exited 1 with
"Failed to create PR". The curl/api_post arm sits a few lines below,
already handles a github upstream, is what auto-pr drives against the same
API every day, and is covered by an existing test. A working fallback that
is never reached is not a fallback.

Found while diagnosing a failed `gh auth login` during the v8.0.0 tag push
-- the same broken binary, one script over.

THIRD INSTANCE OF A NAMED CLASS: INV-rupid ("is the library importable" !=
"are the weights on disk"), WI-dojud ("is the binary present" != "is the
backend on"), now this. The probe asks the caller's real question via `gh
auth status`, the cheapest call exercising the whole path the branch
depends on -- binary, credential, transport. Evaluated ONCE and cached;
putting it in the branch condition would double a network round trip.

It also says WHY it fell back, because a silent fallback reads as the
script ignoring an installed gh.

TESTS, and the differential that makes them non-vacuous: reverting only
the production hunk fails exactly the two new fallback tests (2 failed / 7
passed) and passes them with it (9 passed). The pinning assertion is not
rc==0 but that `pr create` was never ATTEMPTED -- rc==0 alone would also
hold if the gh arm succeeded. A negative control asserts a working gh
still takes the gh arm, without which a probe hardwired to false would
pass every other test here while silently retiring the gh path.

The fake gh gains an `auth status` knob with its OWN variable rather than
reusing GH_EXIT: the existing failure test sets GH_EXIT=1 to make `pr
create` fail, and had the probe shared that knob, that test would have
routed to the curl arm and silently stopped covering the gh failure path
it was written for.

Also adds `--help`. Its absence meant contribute had NO invocation that
did not attempt to create a real upstream PR, which is why a broken
dependency could sit undetected -- the script was only testable by using
it for real.

